==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|bench mark}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|bench|mark}}. Originally (attested circa 1842) a mark cut into a stone by land surveyors to secure a "bench" (from 19th century land surveying jargon, meaning a type of [[bracket]]), to mount measuring equipment. Figurative sense attested circa 1884.<ref>[http://www.etymologie.info/~e/u_/us-manage.html#Benchmark Benchmark etymology on etymologie.info]</ref>

